1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,600
Every cable has a little resistance. Here 0.1 Ω.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:08,900
The loss is P = I² · R. 10 A gives 10 W of heat.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,800
If we double the current, is it twice as hot?

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,900
No! 20 A gives 40 W. Four times as much.

5
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:27,900
Three times the current: nine times the heat. 90 W.

6
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:34,000
A loose terminal has more resistance. That is where it heats up.

7
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:40,900
So we scan panels with a thermal camera, under load, covers on.

8
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,900
Double the current? Four times the heat.
